test_execve — Phase 1 chunk 8 execve() end-to-end
==================================================

Drives `execve()` through the new `PM_CMD_EXECVE` path: this program
calls `pm_execve("/bin/test_exectgt", ["test_exectgt", "ok"])`, which
on success replaces the current process's image with `test_exectgt`
and never returns to this code. `test_exectgt` prints `execve: ok`
and exits, so a successful run produces exactly one matching line.

What this test would fail on if regressed:

  * `svc_proc_replace_image_prep` not draining the old user pages →
    the new ELF's segments would land on top of stale frames whose
    PTEs were never cleared; `vm_install_user_page` would refuse to
    overwrite existing leaves and `load_elf64_to_ptbr` would silently
    fail to write the new code.
  * Per-PT_LOAD VMAs not installed before the SysV stack build →
    a stack-grow fault on the first `push` of a function call would
    miss `vma_handle_fault` (no covering VMA) and trap.
  * `svc_execve_finalize` not writing both `ARCH_FRAME_PC_OFFSET` and
    `ARCH_FRAME_USP_OFFSET` → either the thread iret/eret's into the
    old code (no PC change) or jumps to entry but with the *old* SP
    pointing at a freed page (sp change missed).
  * `svc_execve_finalize` calling the old `ipc_reply` path → the
    1-byte status would `vm_copy_cross` into the new SysV init frame
    and clobber argc or argv[0]'s string data.
  * PM's pre-prep failure handlers running `svc_proc_replace_image_prep`
    before validation finishes → a missing or malformed binary would
    leave the caller's image gone with no way to report the error.

Pass criterion: `test_exectgt` prints `execve: ok\n`. Failure paths
fall through into this program's tail, where we print `execve: bad`
plus the step number we made it to.

    module oskit.bin.test_execve

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, pm_execve}


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("execve: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


Build the argv array as a stack-local string slice and hand it to
`pm_execve`. We pass `argv[0] = "test_exectgt"` (the program name —
SysV ABI convention) and `argv[1] = "ok"` so `test_exectgt` can
verify the argv pipeline survived the address-space replacement.

    main(argv: []string)
        var args: [2]string
        args[0] = "test_exectgt"
        args[1] = "ok"
        val rc = pm_execve("/bin/test_exectgt", &args[0], 2)

If we reach this point execve returned, which means it failed before
the point of no return. `pm_execve` returns -1 on every error path
(file not found, malformed ELF, PM heap exhausted, ...).

        if rc < 0
            fail(1)
            return

`pm_execve` documents that the success path never returns. If the
linker/runtime ever lets control fall through here without the
`exit()` syscall above firing, treat that as a regression: the new
program may have started but resumed inside this code somehow.

        fail(2)
